<!-- BODY -->
<div class="juris-body">
  <div class="juris-main">

    <!-- PLAIN LANGUAGE -->
    <div class="sec-block" style="border-top:none;padding-top:28px;">
      <div class="plain-summary">
        <strong>Iran in plain language:</strong> Iran is an Islamic republic governed by a Supreme Leader who holds absolute religious and political authority above all elected institutions. It is the world's primary state sponsor of terrorism, funding and commanding a network of armed proxy groups — <a href="/org/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a>, <a href="/org/hamas">Hamas</a>, the <a href="/org/houthis">Houthis</a>, and the <span class="term" data-def="The IRGC's external operations directorate, responsible for managing Iran's proxy network across the Middle East. Its HQ was destroyed in the 2026 Iran War.">IRGC Quds Force</span> — across the Middle East. In 2026 it fought and lost a war against Israel and the United States, resulting in the assassination of Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei and severe degradation of its military and proxy network. On non-Muslim citizens: Iran's founding doctrine (<span class="term" data-def="Guardianship of the Islamic Jurist. Iran's founding governance doctrine, developed by Ayatollah Khomeini. Holds that supreme political authority must reside in a qualified Islamic scholar until the return of the Hidden Imam.">Velayat-e Faqih</span>) established Islamic theological supremacy over all civic life. Christians, Jews, and Zoroastrians received constitutional recognition as "People of the Book" — protected but structurally subordinate: barred from the offices of Supreme Leader and President, from full inheritance rights, and from senior military command. <span class="term" data-def="A religious minority in Iran not recognized as 'People of the Book' under the Islamic Republic's constitution. Baha'is have faced systematic state persecution since 1979, including execution, imprisonment, and denial of education and employment.">Baha'is</span>, not recognized as People of the Book, have faced systematic state persecution since 1979. Iran's Jewish community — roughly 80,000–100,000 at the time of the revolution, the largest in the Middle East outside Israel — has contracted to an estimated 8,000–10,000 today. The Islamic Republic's founding documents and state rhetoric conflate Judaism with Zionism, a framing that Khomeini embedded from the outset and successive leaders have maintained.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION: SUCCESSION CRISIS -->
    <div class="sec-block">
      <div class="nh-eyebrow">Active Crisis · Succession</div>
      <h2 class="nh-headline crisis">The Khamenei Succession</h2>
      <p class="nh-lead">On February 28, 2026, Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei was assassinated during the 2026 Iran War — the first death of a sitting Supreme Leader since the founding of the Islamic Republic. His son Mojtaba Khamenei was elected as the third Supreme Leader by the Assembly of Experts on March 9, 2026, following an emergency session. The transition marks the most significant political inflection point in Iran since the 1979 revolution.</p>

      <div class="cb-label"><div class="cb-dot"></div>Post-War Transition · As of June 2026</div>
      <div class="cb-facts">
        <div class="cbf"><div class="cbf-label">Previous Supreme Leader</div><div class="cbf-val">Ali Khamenei (1989–2026 †)</div></div>
        <div class="cbf"><div class="cbf-label">New Supreme Leader</div><div class="cbf-val">Mojtaba Khamenei (Mar 9, 2026–)</div></div>
        <div class="cbf"><div class="cbf-label">Succession Body</div><div class="cbf-val">Assembly of Experts (88 members)</div></div>
        <div class="cbf"><div class="cbf-label">Political Alignment</div><div class="cbf-val">Hardline · IRGC-aligned</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="succession-note">
        <span class="sn-icon">⚠️</span>
        <span class="sn-text">Mojtaba Khamenei is assessed by the Atlantic Council and other analysts as more ideologically hardline than his father, with close ties to extremist clerical factions. He sustained injuries in the airstrike that killed Ali Khamenei and was absent from public view for an extended period after assuming office, creating uncertainty about his capacity to govern.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION: GOVERNANCE STRUCTURE -->
    <div class="sec-block">
      <div class="nh-eyebrow">Government · Dual Authority</div>
      <h2 class="nh-headline">Who Holds Power?</h2>
      <p class="nh-lead">Iran operates a hybrid theocratic-republican system in which elected institutions (President, Parliament) exist beneath an unelected Supreme Leader who holds supreme authority over all branches of government, the military, and the judiciary. The <span class="term" data-def="Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps. Iran's parallel military force, distinct from the conventional army. Commands the Quds Force, Basij, and proxy networks. Answers only to the Supreme Leader. Designated a Foreign Terrorist Organization by the U.S. in 2019.">IRGC</span> operates as a parallel authority layer largely independent of the civilian government.</p>

      <table class="auth-table">
        <thead><tr>
          <th>Office</th><th>Selection</th><th>Current Holder</th><th>Note</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><div class="at-body red">Supreme Leader</div><span class="at-badge ab-unelect">Unelected</span></td>
            <td><div class="at-note">Appointed by Assembly of Experts (elected body)</div></td>
            <td><div class="at-holder">Mojtaba Khamenei</div><div class="at-meta crisis">Since Mar 9, 2026 · ● New</div></td>
            <td><div class="at-note">Commands armed forces, appoints judiciary chief, controls state media and Guardian Council</div></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><div class="at-body">President</div><span class="at-badge ab-vetted">Vetted Election</span></td>
            <td><div class="at-note">Elected by citizens; candidates vetted by Guardian Council</div></td>
            <td><div class="at-holder">Masoud Pezeshkian</div><div class="at-meta">Since Jul 28, 2024</div></td>
            <td><div class="at-note">Heads executive branch; subordinate to Supreme Leader on all strategic matters</div></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><div class="at-body purple">IRGC Commander</div><span class="at-badge ab-nge">Parallel Authority</span></td>
            <td><div class="at-note">Appointed directly by Supreme Leader</div></td>
            <td><div class="at-holder">Hossein Salami</div><div class="at-meta">Incumbent · Multiple deputy commanders killed 2025–26</div></td>
            <td><div class="at-note">Commands ~125,000 ground troops, Quds Force, Basij; answers only to Supreme Leader</div></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><div class="at-body">Guardian Council</div><span class="at-badge ab-unelect">Unelected</span></td>
            <td><div class="at-note">6 clerics appointed by Supreme Leader + 6 jurists nominated by judiciary</div></td>
            <td><div class="at-holder">Ahmad Jannati (Chair)</div><div class="at-meta">Vets all candidates for elected office</div></td>
            <td><div class="at-note">Can disqualify candidates and veto legislation as un-Islamic; effectively controls who may govern</div></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><div class="at-body">Parliament (Majlis)</div><span class="at-badge ab-vetted">Vetted Election</span></td>
            <td><div class="at-note">290 members, 4-year terms; candidates vetted by Guardian Council</div></td>
            <td><div class="at-holder">Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf (Speaker)</div><div class="at-meta">Dominated by hardline factions as of 2024</div></td>
            <td><div class="at-note">Legislative authority; cannot override Supreme Leader or Guardian Council</div></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="circular-lock">⚠ Circular authority lock: The Supreme Leader appoints the Guardian Council, which controls who can be elected, which determines who sits in the Assembly of Experts, which is formally responsible for selecting (and removing) the Supreme Leader. No institution outside the Supreme Leader's network can check this cycle.</div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION: DOCTRINE -->
    <div class="sec-block">
      <div class="nh-eyebrow">Doctrine &amp; Philosophy</div>
      <h2 class="nh-headline">Velayat-e Faqih</h2>
      <p class="nh-lead">Iran's governing philosophy is <span class="term" data-def="Guardianship of the Islamic Jurist. Holds that supreme political authority must reside in a qualified Islamic scholar until the return of the Hidden Imam. Developed by Ayatollah Khomeini and enshrined in Iran's constitution.">Velayat-e Faqih</span> — "Guardianship of the Islamic Jurist" — which holds that supreme political authority must reside in a qualified Islamic scholar until the return of the Hidden Imam. Developed by Ayatollah Khomeini, this doctrine forms the constitutional foundation of the Islamic Republic and explicitly subordinates democracy to theological authority.</p>

      <div class="philosophy-block">
        <div class="pb-eyebrow">Governing Doctrine</div>
        <div class="pb-title">Velayat-e Faqih · وِلایَت فَقیه</div>
        <div class="pb-text">The Supreme Leader holds absolute religious and political authority over the Islamic Republic on behalf of God and the Hidden Imam. Democratic institutions exist but are structurally subordinate. The state's legitimacy derives from divine mandate, not popular consent — though popular consent is used to provide ceremonial validation of this arrangement through controlled elections.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION: AFFILIATED NGES -->
    <div class="sec-block">
      <div class="nh-eyebrow">Foreign Affairs · Axis of Resistance</div>
      <h2 class="nh-headline">Iran's Proxy Network</h2>
      <p class="nh-lead">Iran funds, arms, trains, and commands a network of non-governmental entities across the Middle East, collectively termed the <span class="term" data-def="An Iran-led network of state and non-state actors united by opposition to Israel and U.S. regional influence. Core members: Iran, Hezbollah, Hamas, the Houthis, and the IRGC Quds Force. The 2024–2026 Israeli and U.S. strikes severely degraded the network.">Axis of Resistance</span>. The 2026 Iran War and preceding Israeli strikes significantly degraded this network. Current status reflects post-war conditions as of June 2026.</p>

